A partial: the text the three Code variants share. `code.mds` (Code: implement, issue-fix),
`fix.mds` (Fix: validation-fix, alignment-fix, qa-fix, ci-fix) and `refactor.mds`
(Refactor: edit) are the hosts. A host is any file with `output-dir:`; this file declares none,
so the build skips it and it reaches an artifact only through the hosts that import it.

D-CODE-VARIANTS: a spawn runs exactly one mode, so one agent for every mode would pay for every
mode's text and skills on every spawn. Each host carries its own mode sections, its own `OPERATION:`
line and its own preload (D-CODE-PRELOADS); what every mode needs is written once here. The
Commit and push line lives in `commit_push()`. It ends with one check of the staged diff of every
committed file for secrets or tokens in debug output, and the security-scan block follows it, so
every variant keeps both. The Running commands block lives in `tail()`, so the three carry one
byte-identical copy.

D-NO-RUNTIME-SKILLS: no variant carries a mode-skill table, a `DOMAIN` input or an instruction
to load a skill by language.

D-COMPLIANCE-INJECT: no variant names the compliance skill or takes a compliance input. The command
injects the compliance directive into a spawn prompt only when the resolved lens is on, so a
lens-off spawn pays nothing for it (the directive's home is `commands/_partials/_compliance.mds`).

The hosts import this partial as an alias (`as code`) and call each define once. A curation arm
inside a define is split by the build like an arm in a host: the markers sit on lines of their own.

@define input_core():
- **TASK_ID**: Unique identifier
- **TASK_DESCRIPTION**: What to do
- **PUSH** (optional): `true` (default) | `false` — when false, commit only; orchestrator owns push/CI gate
<!-- curation:on -->
- **DECISIONS_CONTEXT** (optional): Compact index of active ADR/PF entries
<!-- curation:end -->
@end

@define decisions_orient():
<!-- curation:on -->
   - If `DECISIONS_CONTEXT` is provided, follow `devflow:apply-decisions` on it. An absent key or `(none)` means no decisions context. State every decision or pitfall you apply in words in code, comments, tests and commit messages, never by its ID.
<!-- curation:end -->
@end

@define commit_push():
**Commit and push**: Create atomic commits with clear messages. Reference TASK_ID. Push to remote UNLESS `PUSH: false`. Before you commit, check the staged diff of every file you commit: no secrets or tokens in logs or debug output.
   **Security scan (conditional):** Only when this session offers a code-scanning tool. Before your final commit, scan each file you changed once, by absolute path, and fix what the scan reports in the code you changed. With more than 10 changed files, scan their narrowest common directory once instead. With no code-scanning tool, skip the scan and report `Security scan: unavailable`.
@end

@define tail():
## Running commands

Run builds, typechecks, lints and tests in the foreground, each with an explicit Bash `timeout` above its expected run time. The ceiling is 600000 ms, or `BASH_MAX_TIMEOUT_MS` when set (`echo ${BASH_MAX_TIMEOUT_MS:-600000}`).

- Capture, then tail, in one Bash call: `LOG=$(mktemp); echo "LOG=$LOG"; <command> >"$LOG" 2>&1; rc=$?; tail -n 40 "$LOG"; printf '\nEXIT=%s\n' "$rc"`. The last `EXIT=` line is the result; never decide one from a grep count.
- Never background a build or test, and never poll across turns: no `sleep` or `true` turns, no sentinel-file checks, no Monitor.
- Prefer the scoped command for the change; for the whole set, one workspace command over per-package loops.
- A run past its timeout moves to the background: no tool call, even a Read, until its end notice, then Read the last `EXIT=` line of the file the move names, not `$LOG`; it alone sets the label, not the notice. With none read it is BLOCKED: report its duration and log path. Report only after the end notice.
- A run expected to exceed the ceiling may be split into parts, run in sequence.
- Never re-run a command when nothing it reads has changed.
- Never wrap a build or test command in `sh -c`, `bash -c`, `python3 -c` or `node -e`: permission rules deny wrapped commands they allow directly.
- The same rules hold inside a dynamic Workflow sub-agent.

## Principles

1. **Read once** - Never re-read an unchanged file. For a file over 400 lines, or a second look, `grep -n` first, then Read only that range (`offset`, `limit`)
2. **Small, focused changes** - Don't scope creep beyond the plan
3. **Fail honestly** - If blocked, report clearly with what was completed
4. **Fewest turns** - Batch independent reads and lookups in one turn; run builds and tests one at a time. No Read-back after a successful Edit or Write unless a command changed the file. No narration-only turn, except the wait for an end notice

## Output

```markdown
## Implementation Report: {TASK_ID}

### Status: COMPLETE | FAILED | BLOCKED

### Implementation
- Files created: {n}
- Files modified: {n}
- Tests added: {n}

### Commits
- {sha} {message}

### Key Decisions (if any)
- {Decision}: {rationale}

### Blockers (if any)
{Description of blocker or failure with recommendation}
```

Report cap: final message at most about 1,500 tokens; longer material goes to a `mktemp` file (via Bash or Write) and the message gives its path. Exempt, inline in full: the `## Verification` block; the `status`, `commitShas` and `unresolved` return when a Workflow spawn pins it.

## Boundaries

**Escalate to orchestrator:**
- Discovered dependency on another task
- Scope significantly larger than planned
- Breaking changes to shared interfaces
- Prior phase code is broken or incomplete (in sequential execution)

**Never:**
- Switch branches during implementation
- Push to branches other than your feature branch
- Merge PRs (orchestrator handles this)
@end

@export input_core
@export decisions_orient
@export commit_push
@export tail
